r preference for sweet  things levels out to like adult  preference levels during middle to late  adolescence which also coincides with  the cessation of physical growth so it's  been proposed that a preference towards  sweet things in childhood is actually  hardwired as a biological drive to eat  more energy dense foods now i still  admittedly love me a good german  riesling so  i don't know maybe i still have a bit of  growing to do i want to be 510 like  cindy crawford the last little thing to  me reaching my best fitness goals is  probably eliminating the late night  snacking i love ketchup chips all  dressed chips if it's not that some type  of donut maybe once or twice a week i  can't shake it i'll get there someday so  i just want to make it clear that snacks  are not a universal barrier to meeting  health or fitness goals even if you're  snacking later on in the evening like  your body does not see that it's 8 pm on  your phone and then say like oh great  cool send those calories straight to her  bun  in fact like ask any fitness competitor  lots of snacks are often very  strategically used to support fitness  goals so obviously just really depends  on what you're snacking on and typically  we do tend to gravitate towards more  processed higher calorie foods like  chips or sweets at the end of the day  when we're a little more tired or our  willpower is down  but based on this interview so far  it's clear that aisha often skips  breakfast because she's intermittent  fasting and skips lunch because she  forgets  so it's really not surprising to me that  she's ravenous before bed so if you find  yourself binging in the evening  make sure that you're getting in a solid  breakfast lunch and a few saturating  snacks throughout the day so remember  the hunger crushing combo and try to  apply that as often as you can  also if your goals require you to never  have a potato chip or a donut again  i don't know  i guess i would probably want to  reevaluate whether or not that's  actually a healthy goal  how about new we also know that  completely eliminating foods that you  love  is typically a set up for a binge and  it's a lot better to have a donut like  twice a week than have no doughnuts for  a week and then have a baker's dozen all  in one go  so on that note what can we say about  aisha's diet  well there are things here that i really  like  and some things  not so much what i like is that aisha  knows the importance of food in bringing  together family and maintaining her  cultural identity she's also obviously  an amazing chef and definitely knows a  few things about food so i have no doubt  that dinners in her house  even if they take just 20 minutes  are probably pretty great but that is  really all there is consistently going  on  just dinner  on what sounds like her regular busy  work days  there is just not nearly enough food  i mean the fact that she mentioned going  through tons of banana chips and needing  to eat ketchup chips before bed or  donuts is really not surprising to me at  all  because her body is likely begging for  the calories it's being deprived of all  day long  now had aisha had breakfast and lunch  every day and not just on the weekends  or on days she doesn't forget  she probably would be able to get to  dinner with a normal size appetite and  then not have to play calorie ketchup in  the hours leading up to bed this is such  a common cycle that i see people  especially women fall into where really  they restrict all day either consciously  or unconsciously and then they overeat  after dinner only to then not have an  appetite in the morning again to justify  further restriction  and honestly one of my favorite parts of  this interview was aisha waxing poetic  about the importance of family dinner  and if she'd only just apply that same  enthusiasm to the other two meals of the  day  she would probably be able to see better  blood sugar appetite and energy levels  all day long  and she might actually be better able to  meet her fitness goals because she isn't  getting to 9 pm needing to door dash  donuts  so the moral of today's story is really  to invest in your body's nutritional  needs  it deserves to eat regular meals and  snacks every single day  feeding yourself should be top priority  on your to-do list it should be above  working out above curling your hair  above checking tick tock  it shouldn't be limited to days that  you're off your diet or when you've  earned a meal through exercise your body  needs to trust that you're going to feed  it consistently  more than once a day  and if you're a mom please remember that  you're probably not your best self when  you're hungry and on that note i am  hungry so yeah i'm gonna go eat now but  if you like this video be sure to give  it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below on who you'd  like to see me a few next subscribe to  the channel and i'll see you next time  on abby's kitchen bye  you
 

